                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the knowledge flows`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Don't reach for another portal — the evidence and expertise are already in the firm; they simply don't <strong>move</strong> horizontally. Map what knowledge matters (tacit vs. explicit, domain-specific vs. cross-cutting) and where it flows and fails. Through the <strong>communities-of-practice</strong> lens, decide which domains are candidates for cultivation — <em>tacit, cross-unit, evolving</em> knowledge like embedded-security and debugging heuristics — versus which are better served by hierarchy and codified process (stable, explicit, compliance-critical). The trap: rebuilding the documentation portal that earned 9% compliance, or treating every domain the same way.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the knowledge structures`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>€1.2M</strong> across structural options — communities of practice, rotation and shadowing, an expert directory, light digital tools, a codified repository, and an internal conference. Mirror Dupouët's central decision: <em>where to foster self-organising communities versus where to impose hierarchy</em>. Communities move tacit knowledge and spark cross-sector innovation but cannot be commanded into existence; a heavy codified repository enforces consistency yet — as the failed portal showed — cannot manufacture engagement. Match each euro to the knowledge type, and resist the repository reflex.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Cultivate engagement & legitimacy`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Communities die without engagement, sponsorship and protected time — that is exactly where the last initiative failed. Resource credible community leaders, secure management legitimacy <strong>without over-formalising</strong> the community into another reporting bureaucracy, and draw the retiring architects' tacit knowledge into circulation while there's still time. Then the test: a delivery director demands the <em>5% time be cut to 2%</em> to protect a slipping deadline. Hold the line and the communities build momentum; cave and you're back toward the 9%-compliance outcome — except you've spent the budget.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit the learning architecture & pitch the CEO`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Consolidate Rounds 1–3 into a 12-month learning architecture and pitch the CEO who funded the mandate after a €4.2M loss. Commit <strong>quarterly checkpoints</strong> on the four KPIs and a credible <em>structure → engagement → reuse → €-saved</em> chain that recovers a meaningful share of the €11M waste and protects the retiring expertise. The CEO will probe whether the communities survive once the launch energy fades. The trap: promising 'faster learning' with no engagement metric, declaring victory on a launch event, or coercing compliance — which buys a number, not a learning organisation.`,

