                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_domain_right:  `<strong>That's the right classification.</strong> Tacit, cross-unit, evolving knowledge — embedded-security, debugging heuristics, client know-how — is exactly what communities diffuse and repositories cannot. Now match the stable, compliance-critical domains to hierarchy and you have a real map.`,
                    coach_r1_everything:    `<strong>You've made everything a community.</strong> But a voluntary community won't guarantee a DO-178C compliance standard is followed — safety-critical, codified knowledge needs hierarchy's authority. Communities are for the tacit, evolving, cross-cutting domains, not for everything.`,
                    coach_r1_stableonly:    `<strong>You've aimed communities at the stable, codifiable knowledge.</strong> That's backwards — coding standards and compliance checklists are precisely what hierarchy and a repository handle well. Communities earn their keep on the tacit, cross-domain knowledge that won't codify.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's the repository reflex.</strong> Rebuilding the portal first is almost exactly what failed last time at 9% compliance — the rail-signalling knowledge was already archived and nobody found or trusted it. Codification does not move tacit, cross-domain knowledge. Which domains genuinely need a community?`,
                    coach_r1_map_sharp:     `<strong>Sharp map.</strong> You classified the tacit, cross-unit domains for communities AND named the core judgment — match structure to knowledge type. That's the lens that lets you grow communities where they fit and keep hierarchy where consistency is non-negotiable.`,
                    coach_r1_allcommunity:  `<strong>Careful — communities are not always better.</strong> You classified the domains well, but 'self-organising everywhere' breaks down on safety-critical standards where you need enforced, consistent process. Match the structure to the knowledge type.`,
                    coach_r1_allhierarchy:  `<strong>Hierarchy everywhere is how you got here.</strong> Authority and codified process can't manufacture engagement or move tacit knowledge — that's what the 9%-compliance portal proved. The deepest, highest-margin knowledge needs a community's energy, not another mandate.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A community-weighted portfolio — well matched.</strong> You funded the communities and the socialisation (rotation and shadowing) that actually move tacit knowledge, kept the repository light for stable domains, and used directories and tools as enablers. That's structure matched to knowledge type.`,
                    coach_r2_nocommunities: `<strong>You've starved the communities of funding.</strong> Communities of practice are the engine for tacit, cross-unit knowledge — under-fund them and you've built everything except the thing that moves the knowledge that matters.`,
                    coach_r2_repository:    `<strong>That's the repository reflex, at scale.</strong> Pouring the budget into a codified portal with a mandatory workflow is exactly the 2024 model that earned 9% compliance — a headline that nobody used. Tacit, cross-domain knowledge moves through communities, not repositories. Re-weight toward cultivation.`,
                    coach_r2_repoheavy:     `<strong>The repository is taking too much of the budget.</strong> A light, well-scoped repository for stable, compliance-critical knowledge is fine — but the moment it dominates, you're repeating the portal that hit 9%. Shift weight to communities and socialisation.`,
                    coach_r2_conference:    `<strong>A big one-off conference is energy without dynamics.</strong> It feels like momentum, but a single annual event doesn't sustain the participation, identity and shared repertoire that make a community produce knowledge. Fund the ongoing structures, not just the launch party.`,
                    coach_r3_resourced:     `<strong>Resourcing credible leaders is the fuel.</strong> A community runs on participation, identity and a respected coordinator with recognition and protected hours — not on goodwill alone. This is how you cultivate dynamics rather than decree them.`,
                    coach_r3_volunteer:     `<strong>Unresourced leadership starves the community.</strong> 'Lead on top of the day job' means it competes with billable work and loses every time. A credible leader needs recognition and protected hours, or the community meets twice and folds.`,
                    coach_r3_appoint:       `<strong>You've appointed managers and made attendance a report.</strong> That converts a community into hierarchy — Wenger and Dupouët are explicit that participation must be voluntary and identity-driven, or the dynamics die. You've built a reporting line, not a community.`,
                    coach_r3_holdline:      `<strong>You held the line on protected time.</strong> Engineers are 100% billable and rewarded on utilisation; without ring-fenced time the community starves. Defending the 5% against a slipping deadline is exactly the investment the EUR 11M waste justifies.`,
                    coach_r3_partialtime:   `<strong>Some protected time is better than none</strong> — but below the 5% the community struggles to build momentum against billability pressure. The learning investment has a real, defensible cost; protect enough of it to let the dynamics take hold.`,
                    coach_r3_caved:         `<strong>You folded to 2% the moment delivery pushed.</strong> Now the communities have too little time to build momentum and you're back toward the 9%-compliance outcome — except you've spent the budget. When a slipping project always trumps learning, the EUR 11M waste never stops. Where do you hold the line?`,
                    coach_r3_mandated:      `<strong>You've mandated the communities into a bureaucracy.</strong> Fixed attendance and minutes reported to management is a hierarchy wearing a community's name — the voluntary, identity-driven dynamics die. Cultivate with sponsorship and air-cover, don't command.`,
                    coach_r3_nolegit:       `<strong>No sponsorship leaves the community exposed.</strong> Without visible executive air-cover, the first time a manager pulls an engineer back to billable work the community loses — and it has no standing to push back. Legitimacy without over-formalising is the balance.`,
                    coach_r3_documenttacit: `<strong>A template won't capture an architect's judgment.</strong> The retiring architects' deepest expertise is tacit — it lives in how they reason, not in a lessons-learned form. That's the SECI point: tacit knowledge transfers through socialisation — mentoring and shadowing — not documentation.`,
                    coach_r3_ignorearchitects: `<strong>Three architects retire within 18 months.</strong> Leaving their tacit expertise uncaptured is the most expensive thing on this board — once they're gone it's gone. A repository can't reach it; a community with mentoring and shadowing can, but only while there's still time.`,
                    coach_r4_quarterly:     `<strong>Quarterly KPI checkpoints make this accountable.</strong> The CEO funded this after a €4.2M loss and wants proof — leading engagement and reuse data at quarter cadence let you see slippage and correct it long before the 12-month review.`,
                    coach_r4_annual:        `<strong>A single end-of-year number is too slow.</strong> If reuse decays you won't know until the review, when it's too late to correct — and a lone lagging figure is the easiest to game into paper reuse. Commit to leading indicators at quarter cadence.`,
                    coach_r4_promise:       `<strong>'Learning will accelerate' is not a plan.</strong> The CEO wants the structure → engagement → reuse → €-saved chain and quarterly checkpoints, not a promise. Without a metric she disbands the function at month 12.`,
                    coach_r4_relaunch:      `<strong>Re-launching every year treats decay as inevitable.</strong> A campaign-driven community lives on borrowed energy. Build rotating leadership, onboarding rituals and recognition into careers so it sustains itself once the launch energy fades.`,
                    coach_r4_launchenergy:  `<strong>'Momentum will carry it' is exactly what failed before.</strong> Communities decay the moment attention moves on. Without a year-2 plan — rotating leadership, rituals, recognition — this slides back to the 9% baseline and the waste resumes.`,
                    coach_r4_causalchain:   `<strong>That's a fundable case.</strong> A credible structure → engagement → reuse → €-saved chain, quarterly checkpoints and a year-2 sustainment plan show the CEO a mechanism by which knowledge actually flows horizontally — and recovers a real share of the €11M, with the retiring expertise protected.`,
                    coach_r4_coerce:        `<strong>Mandating compliance buys a number, not a learning organisation.</strong> Order sharing and audit hard and reuse rises on paper while engineers go through the motions — and it collapses the moment the pressure lifts. That's the 9% portal with extra steps.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>Culture-change language without targets won't survive the board.</strong> The CEO funded this on the back of a €4.2M loss; she needs a quantified, causal case she can defend — not aspiration.`,

